#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/id #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" :question/math-academy-id "e-5560" :question/problem "For the vector ![](images/b8/b85705767814eae0f99345888b1f0c65a89dbbc76458d7093a02a20937d2e1c3.png) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/85/85cd8d0ebcd70de93ec2bd5007907a02d81daadd27b348e7c72ccef53654d881.png)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/e0/e03fd2577b8d7067f4507d0ee6fe732d3786a494134cd967d3c65de01e28e709.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot 1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)=-6\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H.$\n\n- For the set $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5561" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/d6/d648a1f36d090c680a6b72f30ff447ce78623f746157348f30a02fa4ea245c66.png) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](images/d2/d29f02d59f540cae1aae6057e05eeeb349d84de1ac9be11f6e3b131083f6905a.png) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](images/60/608fd40128068aa5f05e6b256081bd2e0ebbf569fca4c9bae4977740eb4db2ce.png)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](images/d6/d6d7ea3c500d1112c64e19da288fbd7ad7e6ed912cf3121a2292427368b19f0e.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](images/c4/c4875bc998184d934c9b43113cd31091b792f1c04ff4387d4b8ab8a7a08f90d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" :question/math-academy-id "e-5562" :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](images/88/889411800149371acab53c44829a050d69df697ce6723dd95b9045ddf873e484.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](images/ed/ed0e0a3a78ff25be588f62627ffd5bae4074f6c59b8e88ad160e4407253ae6dd.png) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](images/af/af507041d4aa3cc7dfb415afc672b5a7875f9f1a27acced81505797e5f92d7e0.png) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](images/34/34b29c1902a7c441b2ac15be91ffb60b8856ab7dc2706146d09eb02f081827ba.png)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](images/49/496154a4d67c0b7861da038306f1d5cc65188cc036cf8514b24603c7ce4cd948.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "b7cb0b20-e2a6-56d7-b568-ae08c259e581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5913" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace ![](images/be/bee23fc9beaddffe7fd60b2c2bbd2d2ee952a277960c5d08fcad5b934e209f64.png) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](images/ab/abfbcbc8c3b942b93faf4b48336daa54345b97fb21d3aa11685f0d29f206590e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$   \n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-2)+8\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252b8c31-0d1d-5fec-97bf-d410a5a76d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324276} {:answer/id #uuid "91bdd21c-3884-5f46-b4ab-8dea96f936cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325206} {:answer/id #uuid "a42cbaf4-426e-5d63-9b83-f7c29afd84aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325366} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2b6f86-096b-59b1-b532-df21b5b82535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326070} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4a15bf-2013-5ecb-bb8b-43496b9988b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "252b8c31-0d1d-5fec-97bf-d410a5a76d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324276} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc3fce0-7172-5e80-9dbb-cd4c06aeae43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c796dfc-19c9-5431-bc95-d9fa1ccc2efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39623" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efe5e0de-876b-5327-9906-468b6bb4ce4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395900} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b5c424-dcd8-543b-8f0d-5fef2732067d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395901} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c0b411-9dec-5c9e-a4c7-e717d9fd0e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395902} {:answer/id #uuid "c93fce89-b164-5698-86ec-5e8583da03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395903} {:answer/id #uuid "a82b517a-ad12-53fd-947a-2d5015a0fc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c93fce89-b164-5698-86ec-5e8583da03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395903} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e41552-50f9-524e-afed-203ff9c4cd4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43c590fa-d89b-58cd-94e7-3ff278816239" :question/math-academy-id "q-39626" :question/problem "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e540348f-2b1e-50af-b3b2-aada036bca22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133027} {:answer/id #uuid "18de5a94-7860-5626-b010-082769c69b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133028} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae593b8-3731-51fb-b428-c9b5e6ef013d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133029} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb1eadf-72d9-5af5-812e-ed2cd61da4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133030} {:answer/id #uuid "463b5500-f199-56c7-8b6f-9dad96ec289a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133031}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f8bcb0-db74-553b-b49d-9510871b0e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133026}] :question/id #uuid "824158c3-80b8-576d-960c-547ff1a39860" :question/math-academy-id "q-71101" :question/problem "Given that $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$, find the value of $\\frac{b + c}{a}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4730af-deb8-5685-8db4-054a0f26476b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418737} {:answer/id #uuid "45409e4f-d6f7-583a-a654-f6549650ec01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418740} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf16aae-72e1-5107-ae16-5b805fc64b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418742} {:answer/id #uuid "510cc82f-89b0-512e-a913-16359c5c9470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} {:answer/id #uuid "d7982ef7-967e-53d6-ac5f-6caebe695c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "510cc82f-89b0-512e-a913-16359c5c9470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418743} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71e16f6-f24e-58ff-9688-d083942b3ca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95765da4-bd97-53db-8498-26a824409464" :question/math-academy-id "q-80524" :question/problem "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b+c}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05300b3a-c07b-53b5-9aa3-850c659808ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345396} {:answer/id #uuid "07bb4152-0811-533f-9fb0-c0816749cc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345417} {:answer/id #uuid "1e1a8ab7-5f2a-56db-a75a-5079fa93ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345615} {:answer/id #uuid "2833db01-4872-586b-a552-b696d87be2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345713} {:answer/id #uuid "ec614fc1-e82b-5e13-849e-6896b647baf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05300b3a-c07b-53b5-9aa3-850c659808ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eba19c9-5a92-5fe3-9e74-3a6e5457ef6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3a6e8de-174f-5eb2-a5e5-1ab50e3df3b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80576" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ A vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 3=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3=-4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"."}]}
